Docker Question: COPY vs RUN curl — Which One's Faster? 🐳

I was creating a Docker Image of a GitHub Runner for my lab today, I was constantly fixing the errors I wondered:
Should I COPY the actions-runner-linux.tar.gz into the image, or use RUN curl to download it?

💡 Here’s what I learned — and why it matters more than we think.

🆚 COPY vs RUN curl

✅ COPY

- Transfers files from your local machine to the image

- Super fast ⏩ and cache-friendly

- Doesn’t require internet

- Great for reproducible builds


❌ RUN curl/wget

- Downloads files during build time

- Slower — depends on network and server response

- Can break caching (if the file changes or server is slow) # For me it did break the caching 🥲 build was taking to much time.

- Adds a network dependency into your build process


⚡ Result: COPY is faster and more reliable in most cases.

But here’s the catch…

If your file is versioned externally or changes frequently (like downloading the latest release of a tool), RUN curl still has its place.

🔍 Lesson: Use COPY for speed and stability. Use curl only when dynamic download is unavoidable
